input-selector: release active_sinkpad_lock around the srcpad push

gst_selector_pad_chain() takes active_sinkpad_lock in reader mode at
entry and only releases it at the very end of the function (the
`done:` label), spanning the call to gst_pad_push() that forwards the
buffer to a downstream sink. For a synced subtitle sink this push can
block for a long, unbounded time waiting for a sparse cue's display
time.

Any writer that needs to commit a pending active-pad switch
(gst_input_selector_maybe_commit_active_pad(), triggered from e.g. a
flushing seek's FLUSH_START via gst_input_selector_event()) has to
wait for that reader to release the lock first. With the reader stuck
in a downstream sink's clock wait, the writer -- and therefore the
thread issuing the seek -- blocks for as long as that wait takes,
which for a sparse external subtitle stream can be many seconds or
longer. Confirmed via full-thread wchan/backtrace dump: the seeking
thread parks on the writer-lock futex while the streaming thread for
the active text pad sits in the sink's clock wait, both blocked on
active_sinkpad_lock. Matches upstream issue #5150.

Every other blocking wait in this file (gst_input_selector_wait_
running_time(), the recursive gst_selector_pad_chain() call used to
flush cached buffers) already releases active_sinkpad_lock before
blocking and reacquires it after. The final push in
gst_selector_pad_chain() is the one place that does not.

Release the reader lock around the push, mirroring the pattern already
used everywhere else in this file. Since the active pad can now change
while we're unlocked, only mark this pad as having pushed if it is
still the active one when we regain the lock -- the same guard the
single-mutex design used before the two-phase active-pad switch was
introduced (dd0c2e3ebadb "input-selector: implement a two-phase
sinkpad switch").

This is a minimal, targeted fix: it does not touch the pending/commit
machinery, EOS handling, allocation-query handling or active-pad
disappearance handling added by the two-phase rework and its five
follow-up fixes -- only the one lock scope that can deadlock against a
concurrent seek.

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [downstream deadlock workaround for
https://gitlab.freedesktop.org/gstreamer/gstreamer/-/issues/5150,
narrower than reverting the two-phase switch outright. Revisit if
upstream fixes the lock scope itself.]

--- a/plugins/elements/gstinputselector.c
+++ b/plugins/elements/gstinputselector.c
@@ -1266,7 +1266,16 @@ gst_selector_pad_chain (GstPad * pad, GstObject * parent, GstBuffer * buf)

   GST_INPUT_SELECTOR_UNLOCK (sel);

+  /* Do not hold active_sinkpad_lock across the push: gst_pad_push() can
+   * block for a long, unbounded time inside a synced sink (e.g. a subtitle
+   * sink waiting for a sparse cue's display time), and holding a reader
+   * lock for that whole span starves any writer trying to commit a pending
+   * active-pad switch (gst_input_selector_maybe_commit_active_pad(), called
+   * e.g. from a flushing seek's FLUSH_START), deadlocking it against this
+   * chain function. See https://gitlab.freedesktop.org/gstreamer/gstreamer/-/issues/5150 */
+  g_rw_lock_reader_unlock (&sel->active_sinkpad_lock);
   res = gst_pad_push (sel->srcpad, buf);
+  g_rw_lock_reader_lock (&sel->active_sinkpad_lock);
   GST_LOG_OBJECT (pad, "Buffer %p forwarded result=%d", buf, res);

   GST_INPUT_SELECTOR_LOCK (sel);
@@ -1277,7 +1286,10 @@ gst_selector_pad_chain (GstPad * pad, GstObject * parent, GstBuffer * buf)
     gst_input_selector_cleanup_old_cached_buffers (sel, pad);
   }

-  selpad->pushed = TRUE;
+  /* the active pad may have been committed to a different sinkpad while we
+   * were unlocked above; only mark this pad as pushed if it still is one */
+  if (sel->active_sinkpad == pad)
+    selpad->pushed = TRUE;

   GST_INPUT_SELECTOR_UNLOCK (sel);
